{"subjectId":"python","id":"modules-packages","title":"Modules, Packages & Environments","group":"Advanced Python","order":22,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A module is a <code>.py</code> file; a package is a folder of modules. <code>import</code> finds, runs and caches them — and virtual environments keep each project's dependencies separate.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-download\"></i>Importing</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import math                          # whole module\nfrom collections import Counter      # one name\nimport datetime as dt                # alias\nprint(math.sqrt(16), Counter(\"aab\")[\"a\"], dt.date(2026, 1, 1).year)   # 4.0 2 2026</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Find</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Python searches <code>sys.path</code>: the script's folder, <code>PYTHONPATH</code>, the standard library, then <code>site-packages</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Execute once</div><div class=\"step-desc\">The module's top-level code runs the first time it is imported.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Cache</div><div class=\"step-desc\">The module object is stored in <code>sys.modules</code>; later imports reuse it.</div></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-player-play\"></i><code>if __name__ == \"__main__\":</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># utils.py\ndef add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":    # True only when run directly: python utils.py\n    print(add(2, 3))          # 5</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>When the file is imported, <code>__name__</code> is <code>\"utils\"</code>, so the demo code doesn't run. Use this guard for scripts, tests and anything with side effects.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-folders\"></i>Packages</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Layout</figcaption><pre><code>shop/\n├── __init__.py        # marks a regular package; runs on \"import shop\"\n├── cart.py\n└── payments/\n    ├── __init__.py\n    └── upi.py\n\nfrom shop.cart import Cart            # absolute import\nfrom .cart import Cart                # relative import (inside the package)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box-multiple\"></i>Virtual environments &amp; pip</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Terminal</figcaption><pre><code>$ python3 -m venv .venv               # create\n$ source .venv/bin/activate           # activate (Windows: .venv\\Scripts\\activate)\n(.venv) $ pip install requests        # install into this project only\n(.venv) $ pip freeze &gt; requirements.txt\n(.venv) $ pip install -r requirements.txt\n(.venv) $ deactivate</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield\"></i>Why venvs</h3><p>Each project gets its own package versions — no conflicts, reproducible installs.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-settings\"></i><code>pyproject.toml</code></h3><p>The modern single file for project metadata and dependencies (used by pip, uv, Poetry).</p></div></div></section>"}